<?xml version="1.0"?>
<?xml-model href="http://download.ros.org/schema/package_format3.xsd" schematypens="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"?>
<package format="3">
  <name>selene_agent</name>
  <version>0.1.0</version>
  <description>Per-robot autonomy stack for SELENE lunar ISRU fleet</description>
  <maintainer email="jushoya@selene.dev">JusHoya</maintainer>
  <license>Apache-2.0</license>

  <depend>rclpy</depend>
  <depend>selene_msgs</depend>
  <depend>selene_hal</depend>
  <depend>geometry_msgs</depend>
  <depend>nav_msgs</depend>
  <depend>sensor_msgs</depend>
  <depend>builtin_interfaces</depend>
  <depend>lifecycle_msgs</depend>

  <!--
    RUNTIME DATA DEPENDENCY ON selene_sim, DELIBERATELY NOT DECLARED.

    As of 2026-08-01 (deviation D-28) `agent_node` reads the committed lunar
    heightmap at start-up: `OccupancyGrid.from_config` loads it through
    `selene_agent/selene_agent/terrain_slope.py` so the planner can enforce
    `navigation.max_traversable_slope_deg`. The data lives in
    `share/selene_sim/models/lunar_terrain/heightmaps`.

    It is NOT an <exec_depend> because the reverse edge already exists:
    `selene_sim/launch/unified_sim.launch.py` resolves
    `FindPackageShare('selene_agent')` to start this package's agents. Declaring
    selene_agent -> selene_sim as well would make that pair a cycle. The data
    dependency is one-way and real; the PACKAGE dependency would not be.

    How it is resolved, most explicit first (see `terrain_slope.find_heightmap_dir`):
    $SELENE_TERRAIN_HEIGHTMAPS, then selene_sim's ament index entry, then a walk
    up from the source checkout. If none of them holds the data the agent raises
    `TerrainDataUnavailable` and does not start — chosen over continuing on
    assumed-flat ground, which is D-28 itself and is invisible until a hauler
    pins on a wall.
  -->

  <test_depend>ament_copyright</test_depend>
  <test_depend>ament_flake8</test_depend>
  <test_depend>ament_pep257</test_depend>
  <test_depend>python3-pytest</test_depend>

  <export>
    <build_type>ament_python</build_type>
  </export>
</package>
